<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.2.2: Multiplying Binomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01592</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.2.2: Multiplying Binomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ea7a5b8b-fc79-4378-8674-3a2aca4604f3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">The binomials in the expression \((x-7)(2x+4)\) are solved three different ways in the table. Describe how the two binomials are being multiplied. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00001">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Description of Solution.</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00002">\((x-7)(2x+4)\)</para>
                    <para id="para-00003">\(x(2x+4)-7(2x+4)\)</para>
                    <para id="para-00004">\(2x^2+4x-14x-28\)</para>
                    <para id="para-00005">\(2x^2-10x-28\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00006"><emphasis>First,</emphasis> ______________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00007"><emphasis>Second,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00008"><emphasis>Next,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00009">So, the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00010">
              \(\begin{align*}
              x &amp;- 7 \\
              \underline{\times \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;2x}&amp;\underline{~+ 4\;\;\;} \\
              4x &amp;- 28\\
              \underline{2x^2 -14x} &amp;  \underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;}\\
              2x^2 - 10x &amp;-28 \\
              \end{align*}\)
          </para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00011"><emphasis>First,</emphasis> ______________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00012"><emphasis>Second,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00013"><emphasis>Third,</emphasis> ____________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00014"><emphasis>Next,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00015">So, the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00016">
                                      <media alt="IMAGE SHOWING MULTIPLICATION OF THE BINOMIALS, WITH AN ARROW GOING FROM X TO 2X, AN ARROW GOING FROM X TO 4, AN ARROW GOING FROM NEGATIVE 7 TO 2X, AND AN ARROW GOING FROM NEGATIVE 7 TO 4.">
                          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_03_01.jpg"/>
                        </media>
                      
                  </para>
                    <para id="para-00017">\(2x^2+4x-14x-28\)</para>
                    <para id="para-00018">\(2x^2-10x-28\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00019"><emphasis>First,</emphasis> ______________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00020"><emphasis>Second,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00021"><emphasis>Third,</emphasis> ____________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00022"><emphasis>Next,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
                    <para id="para-00023">Combine like terms, and the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para id="para-00024">Write down your answers, then discuss your steps with a partner. Do you agree on all of the steps? If not, make changes where needed.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">When you have finished, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00026">Compare your answers:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00002">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>Description of Solution.</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00027">\((x-7)(2x+4)\)</para>
                    <para id="para-00028">\(x(2x+4)-7(2x+4)\)</para>
                    <para id="para-00029">\(2x^2+4x-14x-28\)</para>
                    <para id="para-00030">\(2x^2-10x-28\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>First</emphasis>, the second binomial \((2x + 4)\) is distributed to both of the terms in the first binomial.</para>
                    <para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Second</emphasis>, individual terms are distributed.</para>
                    <para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Next</emphasis>, the like terms \(4x\) and \(–14x\) are combined.</para>
                    <para id="para-00034">So, the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00035">
            \(\begin{align*}
            x &amp;- 7 \\
            \underline{\times \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;2x}&amp;\underline{~+ 4\;\;\;} \\
            4x &amp;- 28\\
            \underline{2x^2 -14x} &amp;  \underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;}\\
            2x^2 - 10x &amp;-28 \\
            \end{align*}\)
        </para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00036"><emphasis>First</emphasis>, the binomials are written vertically.</para>
                    <para id="para-00037"><emphasis>Second</emphasis>, the last term of the bottom binomial is multiplied, or distributed, to each of the terms in the top binomial.</para>
                    <para id="para-00038"><emphasis>Third</emphasis>, the first term of the bottom binomial is multiplied, or distributed, to each of the terms in the top binomial, being sure to line up the like terms vertically.</para>
                    <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Next</emphasis>, add the like terms. They should be “stacked” on top of each other.</para>
                    <para id="para-00040">So, the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00041">
                                      <media alt="IMAGE SHOWING MULTIPLICATION OF THE BINOMIALS, WITH AN ARROW GOING FROM X TO 2X, AN ARROW GOING FROM X TO 4, AN ARROW GOING FROM NEGATIVE 7 TO 2X, AND AN ARROW GOING FROM NEGATIVE 7 TO 4.">
                          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_03_02.jpg"/>
                        </media>
                      
                  </para>
                    <para id="para-00042">\(2x^2+4x-14x-28\)</para>
                    <para id="para-00043">\(2x^2-10x-28\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>First</emphasis>, distribute the first term of the front binomial to the first term of the back binomial (F).</para>
                    <para id="para-00045"><emphasis>Second</emphasis>, distribute the first term of the front binomial to the second term of the back binomial (O).</para>
                    <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Third</emphasis>, distribute the second term of the front binomial to the first term of the back binomial (I).</para>
                    <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Next</emphasis>, distribute the second term of the front binomial to the second term of the back binomial (L).</para>
                    <para id="para-00048">Combine like terms, and the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para id="para-00049">The video below can also help break down solutions if you find yourself stuck.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f7497b4b5abbe1e66c6824a9d34edeed4d604ee4"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00050">Multiply each binomial. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00051">\((a+6)(a-4)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00052">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00053">\(a^2+2a-24\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00054">\((5b+9)(4b+3)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00055">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00056">\(20b^2+51b+27\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00057">\((5x^2-1)(2x-8)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00058">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00059">\(10x^3-40x^2-2x+8\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00060">\((7mn+1)(11mn-3)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00061">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00062">\(77m^2n^2-10mn-3\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise></section>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
          <section id="sect-00003">          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>

      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00063">Multiply each binomial. Add like terms to simplify the expression. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
          <para id="para-00064">\((5x^2y-xy)(3x+y)+(6x+4y)(-3x^2y+2xy)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00065">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00066">\((5x^2y-xy)(3x+y)+(6x+4y)(-3x^2y+2xy)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00067">\(\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;=15x^3y+5x^2y^2-3x^2y-xy^2-18x^3y+12x^2y-12x^2y^2+8xy^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00068">\(\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;=-3x^3y+9x^2y-7x^2y^2+7xy^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
  </section>

    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001"><para id="para-00069">Multiply the binomials.</para>
      <para id="para-00070">\((5y+2)(6y-3)\)</para></note>

      <section id="sect-00004"><title>Additional Resources</title>
        <section id="sect-00005">
          <title>Multiplying Binomials Using the Distributive Property</title>
          <para id="para-00071">Just like there are different ways to represent multiplication of numbers, there are several different ways to represent how to multiply a binomial times a binomial using the Distributive Property of Multiplication over Addition.</para>
          <para id="para-00072">Distribute to multiply the binomials.</para>
          <para id="para-00073">
           <emphasis>Example 1</emphasis>
        </para>
          <para id="para-00074">\((y+5)(y+8)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00075"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Distribute the binomial \((y+8)\).</para>
          <para id="para-00076">
                  <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_01.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00077"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Now, distribute the individual terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00078"> \(y^2+8y+5y+40\)</para>
          <para id="para-00079"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00080"> \(y^2+13y+40\)</para>
          <para id="para-00081">
           <emphasis>Example 2</emphasis>
        </para>
          <para id="para-00082">\((4y^2+3)(2y^2-5)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00083"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Distribute the binomial \((2y^2 - 5)\).</para>
          <para id="para-00084">\(4y^2{\style{color:red}(}{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}−}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red})}+ 3(2y^2−5) \)</para>
          <para id="para-00085"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Distribute the individual terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00086">\(8y^2-20y^2+6y^2-15\)</para>
          <para id="para-00087"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00088">\(8y^4-14y^2-15\)</para>
        </section>
        <section id="sect-00006">
          <title>Multiplying Binomials Using the FOIL Method</title>
          <para id="para-00089">If you multiply binomials often enough, you may notice a pattern. Notice that the first term in the result is the product of the <emphasis>first</emphasis> terms in each binomial. The second and third terms are the product of multiplying the two <emphasis>outer</emphasis> terms and then the two <emphasis>inner</emphasis> terms. And the last term results from multiplying the two <emphasis>last</emphasis> terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00090">We abbreviate “First, Outer, Inner, Last” as FOIL. The letters stand for “First, Outer, Inner, Last.” We use this as another way to multiply binomials. The word FOIL is easy to remember and ensures we find all four products.</para>
          <para id="para-00091">Let’s multiply \((x+3)(x+7)\) using both methods.</para>
          <para id="para-00092">
                  <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_02.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00093">We summarize the steps of the FOIL method below. The FOIL method only applies to multiplying binomials, not other polynomials!</para>
          <note id="note-00002">
            <para id="para-00094">
             <emphasis> How to use the FOIL method to multiply two binomials:</emphasis>
          </para>
            
            <para id="para-00095"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Multiply the <emphasis>First</emphasis> terms.</para>
            <para id="para-00096"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Multiply the <emphasis>Outer</emphasis> terms.</para>
            <para id="para-00097"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Multiply the <emphasis>Inner</emphasis> terms.</para>
            <para id="para-00098"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Multiply the <emphasis>Last</emphasis> terms.</para>
            <para id="para-00099"><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Combine like terms, when possible.</para>
            <para id="para-00100">
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </note>
          <para id="para-00101">When you multiply by the FOIL method, drawing the arrows will help your brain focus on the pattern and make it easier to apply.</para>
          <para id="para-00102">Now we will review examples where we use the FOIL pattern to multiply two binomials.</para>
          <para id="para-00103">Multiply the binomials using the FOIL method.</para>
          <para id="para-00104">
           <emphasis>Example 3</emphasis>
        </para>
          <para id="para-00105">\((y-7)(y+4)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00106"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Multiply the \({\style{color:red}F}{\style{color:red}i}{\style{color:red}r}{\style{color:red}s}{\style{color:red}t}\) terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00107">
                  <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_04.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00108"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Multiply the \({\style{color:red}O}{\style{color:red}u}{\style{color:red}t}{\style{color:red}e}{\style{color:red}r}\) terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00109">
                  <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_05.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00110"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Multiply the \({\style{color:red}I}{\style{color:red}n}{\style{color:red}n}{\style{color:red}e}{\style{color:red}r}\) terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00111">
                  <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_06.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00112"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Multiply the \({\style{color:red}L}{\style{color:red}a}{\style{color:red}s}{\style{color:red}t}\) terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00113">
                  <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_07.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00114"><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00115"> \(y^2 - 3y - 28\)</para>
          <para id="para-00116">
           <emphasis>Example 4</emphasis>
        </para>
          <para id="para-00117">\((4x+3)(2x-5)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00118">
                  <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_08.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00119"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Multiply the <emphasis>First</emphasis> terms, \(
      {\style{color:red}4}
      {\style{color:red}x} \;
      {\style{color:red}\cdot} \; {\style{color:red}2}
      {\style{color:red}x}
      \).</para>
          <para id="para-00120">
                  <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_09.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00121"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Multiply the <emphasis>Outer</emphasis> terms, \(
      {\style{color:red}4}
      {\style{color:red}x}\;
      {\style{color:red}\cdot}\;
      {\style{color:red}(}
      {\style{color:red}{-5}}
      {\style{color:red})}
      \).</para>
          <para id="para-00122">
                  <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_10.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00123"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Multiply the <emphasis>Inner</emphasis> terms, \(
      {\style{color:red}3}\;
      {\style{color:red}\cdot}\;
      {\style{color:red}2}
      {\style{color:red}x}
      \).</para>
          <para id="para-00124">
                  <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_11.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00125"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Multiply the <emphasis>Last</emphasis> terms, \(
      {\style{color:red}3}\;
      {\style{color:red}\cdot}\;
      {\style{color:red}(}
      {\style{color:red}{-5}}
      {\style{color:red})}
      \).</para>
          <para id="para-00126">
              <media alt=".">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_05_05_12.jpg"/>
              </media>
        </para>
          <para id="para-00127"><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.
      \(8x^2-14x-15\)</para>
          <para id="para-00128">The FOIL method is usually the quickest method for multiplying two binomials, but it <emphasis>only</emphasis> works for binomials. You can use the Distributive Property to find the product of any two polynomials.</para>
          </section>
          <section id="sect-00007">
          <title>Multiplying Binomials Using Vertical Alignment</title>
          <para id="para-00129">Another representation that works for all polynomials is Vertical Alignment. It is much like the method you use to multiply whole numbers. Look carefully at this example of multiplying two-digit numbers.</para>
          <para id="para-00130">\(\underline{\underline{\;\;\;\;23\\\times46}\\\;\;138\;\;\;\mathrm{partial}\;\mathrm{product}\\\;\;920\;\;\;\mathrm{partial}\;\mathrm{product}}\\1058\;\;\;\mathrm{product}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00131">Start by multiplying 23 by 6 to get 138.
      Next, multiply 23 by 4, lining up the partial product in the correct columns.
      Use a 0 as a place holder in the ones column because you will be multiplying by 4 tens.</para>
          <para id="para-00132">Finally, add the partial products.</para>
          <para id="para-00133">
           <emphasis>Example 5</emphasis>
        </para>
          <para id="para-00134">Multiply using Vertical Alignment: \((3y-1)(2y-6)\). It does not matter which binomial goes on the top.</para>
          <para id="para-00135"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Multiply \(3y-1\) by \(-6\).</para>
          <para id="para-00136"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Multiply \(3y-1\) by \(2y\).</para>
          <para id="para-00137"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Add like terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00138">Notice the partial products are the same as the terms in the FOIL method.</para>
          <para id="para-00139">\(\begin{array}{l}\begin{array}{r}\;\;\left(3y-1\right)\\\underline{\times\left(2y-6\right)}\\{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}8}{\style{color:red}y}\;{\style{color:red}+}\;{\style{color:red}6}\\{\style{color:blue}6}{\style{color:blue}y}^{\style{color:blue}2}\;{\style{color:blue}-}\;{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\\\hline6y^2-20y+6\end{array}\end{array}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00140">We have now used three ways to multiply binomials. Be sure to practice each process, and try to decide which one you prefer.</para>
          <note class="try" id="note-00003">
      <title>Try It: Multiplying Binomials</title>
          
            <exercise id="exer-00007">
              <problem id="prob-00007">
                <para id="para-00141">In questions 1 - 2, multiply the binomials using any of the methods described.</para>
                <para id="para-00142">\((x+8)(x+9)\)</para>
                <para id="para-00143">Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
              </problem>
              <solution id="sol-00007">
                <para id="para-00144">Compare your answer: You answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
                <para id="para-00145">
                 <emphasis>Using the Distributive Property by distributing either a binomial or an individual term:</emphasis>
              </para>
                <para id="para-00146">\((x+8)(x+9)\) 
        \(x(x+9)+8(x+9)\) 
        \(x^2+9x+8x+72\) 
        \(x^2+17x+72\)</para>
                <para id="para-00147">
                 <emphasis>Using FOIL:</emphasis>
              </para>
                <para id="para-00148">\((x+8)(x+9)\)</para>
                <para id="para-00149">\(x^2+9x+8x+72\)
        F       
        O      
        I        
        L</para>
                <para id="para-00150">\(x^2+17x+72\)</para>
                <para id="para-00151">
                 <emphasis>Using Vertical Alignment:</emphasis>
              </para>
                <para id="para-00152">\(\underline{\underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x+8\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\times\;x+9}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;9x+72\\ \;\;x^2+8x}\\x^2+17x+72\)</para>
              </solution>
            </exercise>
          
          
            <exercise id="exer-00008">
              <problem id="prob-00008">
                <para id="para-00153">\((3c^2+4)(5c-2)\)</para>
                <para id="para-00154">Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
              </problem>
              <solution id="sol-00008">
                <para id="para-00155">Compare your answer: You answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
                <para id="para-00156">
                 <emphasis>Using the Distributive Property by distributing either a binomial or an individual term:</emphasis>
              </para>
                <para id="para-00157">\((3c^2+4)(5c-2)\) 
            \(3c^2(5c-2)+4(5c-2)\) 
            \(15c^3-6c^2+20c-8\)</para>
                <para id="para-00158">
                 <emphasis>Using FOIL:</emphasis>
              </para>
                <para id="para-00159">\((3c^2+4)(5c-2)\)</para>
                <para id="para-00160">\(15c^3-6c^2+20c-8\)
      F           
      O          
      I       
      L</para>
                <para id="para-00161">\(15c^3-6c^2+20c-8\)</para>
                <para id="para-00162">
                 <emphasis>Using Vertical Alignment:</emphasis>
              </para>
                <para id="para-00163">\(\underline{\underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;3c^2+4\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\times5c-2}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;-6c^2\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-8\\15c^3\;\;\;\;\;\;\;+20c}\\15c^3-6c^2+20c-8\)</para>
              </solution>
            </exercise>
          </note>
        </section>
      </section>

